## Chapter 1.5: Prime Suspect

It certainly was not easy to accept. If what Luka deduced was true then he might have just exposed the true perpetrator’s ultimate plan: Igor wasn’t dead and the charred corpse everyone assumed was him was someone else. Could this be the work of Igor himself? Or his assailant, perhaps? But to what end? And who was this poor sod that unfortunately took his place? Thousands more questions rushed into his mind until…

“Luka… Hey… Luka!” A familiar voice seeped through.

Luka snapped back to reality to react to the sound. The vision slowly faded in. Nadya was standing right in front of him, behind the cordoning tape, waving her hand in front of his face.

“A- Ah. Yeah?” Luka shook his head to clear the after-image.

“Used your _vision_ again, haven’t you?” She teased, still deeply skeptical of his shtick.

“Yeah… Sorry. What you was about to say?” Luka tried to be polite and decided to not let his condition to of her worries.

“No, no, it was nothing. I mean, I already saw the crime scene, my boss already bit my head off,” she made a sarcastic gestures, “So, I’m about to return to the station. Wanna join?”

“Uh- Sure! I’ve already seen enough…” Luka still was unsure whether he should have just let this go or not.

Upon hearing his confirmation, Nadya ducked under the tape to meet him and walk ahead out of the crowd. Once they exit the busy zone, Luka just couldn’t keep his mouth shut anymore.

“Hey, Nadya.” Luka started.

“Yeah?” Nadya slowed her pace, letting him catch up alongside her.

“May I ask you a favor?”

“Sure, shoot.”

“Do you have a way to look up someone?”

Nadya giggled, “Come now, abusing authoritative power already? What’s the—” she halted herself mid question. She dragged Luka all around the place for long enough, she needed to show some gratitude.

“You know what? I won’t ask. What’s the detail?” she asked while un-clipping her field pocket terminal[footnote:fr6dv] from her winter coat.

%Footnote.fr6dv: _Spravka-M_ (Справка-М [IPA_ru:ˈsprafkə ɛm]): An encrypted, pocket-sized tactical inquiry terminal issued to Soviet law enforcement officers. Encased in ruggedized, glove-friendly polycarbonate with a high-contrast e-paper display, it interfaces directly with district civil registries and local precinct databases via secure burst radio packets.

“I have a name: Anatoley Viktorovich Ruzhin” he said it slowly as he did his best to recall the name from his vision.

She repeated what he said to her for confirmation before saying, “Right. Let me look it up,” she promptly unlocked the terminal and spoke to it, using its voice command feature, “Dezhurny[footnote:fjhf9], look up a citizen in this district named, Anatoly—” as she said the given name, Luka interrupted.

%Footnote.fjhf9: _Dezhurny_ (Дежурный [IPA_ru:dʲɪˈʐurnɨj]): Literally “Duty Officer” (derived from French _de jour_). On the _Spravka-M_ terminal, it refers to the lightweight, quantized offline artificial intelligence assistant that parses natural voice and text queries into structured municipal registry searches.

“It’s Anatoley with an ‘ey’,” he clarified.

Nadya looked at him and chuckled, “With an ‘ey’? Pfttt. That’s so… 21st century.”

Luka shrugged, that was what written on the headstone. Parents named their child with variation in the spelling to make them _standout_ all the time.

Nadya reactive the voice command again, this time with an exact spelling of the name. In a blink of an eye, the AI assistant displayed the queried data on the screen. Nadya however, did not prepare for this shocking result.

On her screen, the result returned a single entry. One and only that displayed letter-by-letter of the name that Luka gave her. Was it just pure luck? Or maybe he was pulling prank on her?

“…I would assume that you didn’t know this person, right?” She asked a clarifying question, hoping that his response would make this make sense.

He shook his head in sincerity. Luka face was clearly tense up from seeing her reaction. He also didn’t expect this.

“T-Then how did you—”

“The vision,” Luka abruptly answered before she could finish, “I looked at the corpse and I saw some headstones… Anatoley died exactly today,” he shared his deduction with a weary voice.

Chills went down Nadya spine making for visibly tense up, her hand clutch the terminal tight, her lips trembled. The look on Luka’s face was the same look he had when he told her Alex’s _crime in the future_. It was then that her doubt in Luka had completely vanished, not because of their bond or trust but because of an overwhelming material evidence that was impossible to deny.

“E-Excuse me, do you mind waiting around here? I-I need to report this finding to my superior,” she said with such nervousness and panic. Luka nodded in confirmation before she rushed back to the crime scene again.

Nadya wasn’t the only one that felt like the atmosphere was ten times heavier. When he decided to report Alex to the Militsiya, he was already one foot out of the door, but deep down, he still clung to a sliver of hope; a hope that perhaps his vision was just a rare mental condition, something that wasn’t the truth. 

Maybe he worked with Alex to much he started seeing things. Maybe his mind subconsciously noticed the plaque on Nadya’s desk, so he can tell her name. Those two visions he saw still wasn’t too outlandish, if it turned out to be just a hallucination.

But this time, it wasn’t possible to see it that way anymore. There was no way that he would know a stranger’s name letter-by-letter down to the lineage name. The weird spelling of Anatoley name cemented it ever more. This was, no doubt, a **premonition**.

Normally, as a theorist, a breakthrough like this is a cause for celebration. His skepticism had been proven correct. However, that would mean that he had lost all the deniabilities that Alex’s crime wasn’t real. That was his worst fear; Alex’s crime was now imminent and he had no clue how to even deal with it.

With two feet out of the door, the biting wind of truth had never been so cold. 

Luka found a place to sit nearby and contemplated this new reality, staring blankly into the snow and the crowd for many minutes.

Then the crowd parted to give way for a group of officers, one of them being Nadya. The leader of group seemed to be this serious and stoic officer. They were heading toward Luka once she pointed him out.

Luka smelled a trouble brewing in the air but it was too late for him to do anything now. All he could do is to prepare for this confrontation.

The stoic officer approached him, “You are Luka Huo, correct?”

Luka slowly stood up to meet his gaze, “Yes, Comrade Officer.”

The officer nodded then offered him a professional handshake, “Citizen Huo, I’m Senior Investigator, Major Tarasov, Viktor Danilovich.”

Luka reached for a cold and awkward handshake. Then he continued,

“Officer Morozova reported that you discovered an important clue about the investigation. She already told me all the details, so I’m here to ask you a single question:” his gaze was fixed on Luka, stare was intense,

“Are you sure that haven’t met Citizen Ruzhin before?”

It’s the same question that Nadya asked him just before the revelation hit her. Glancing at Nayda to his left, her face was a mixed of worry and awkwardness. At this point it was too late to play it off as a prank. He only had one choice,

“Yes, I’m sure, Comrade Major.”

Tarasov looked to the side, nodding to himself slowly before turning his gaze sharply back on Luka, “In that case, Citizen Huo, I’m afraid that you have become our prime suspect. Please follow us to the station.”

His command was crisp, cold, and concise. Before Luka could object, Nadya immediately interrupted, “C-Comrade Major! Comrade Huo is our witness, not suspect! His vision revealed—”

He turned sharply to Nadya in a profound frustration, “Morozova! I know you are young, naive, and innocent but do I have to spell it out to you!?” he shut her down and pointed at Luka, “Listen. This man is using you to get to the crime scene!”

Nadya froze. She hadn’t give this proposition a thought. She glanced at Luka and thought about the entire morning. In a way, it makes sense. If he really was the culprit then he had gotten very close to the crime scene without a glimpse of her suspicion.

With an overwhelming confusion and conflicting thoughts, Nadya decided to stand down, “Understood, Comrade Major…”

Tarasov turned back to Luka, “Now, I don’t know what kind of game are you playing with this _vision_ of yours. But I’ll let you know that this is Militsiya. Not conspiracy theorists. We don’t believe in _premonition_,” he gave a sarcastic glance to Nadya.

It seemed that only Luka and Nadya believed it. But who could blame him? Even Luka didn’t fully believe it until now.

“…Am I under arrest, Comrade Major?” Luka asked one clarifying question, an answer to that will decide his fate.

“No,” he let out a direct answer, “We don’t have a probable cause. You are lucky that we don’t take your _vision_ seriously, at least for now.” 

He continued, “though, I have a reasonable suspicion to place you under investigation and issue you a municipal travel ban.[footnote:fhgc1]”

%Footnote.fhgc1: _Podpiska o nevyezde_ (Подписка о невыезде [IPA_ru:pɐtˈpʲiskə ɐ nʲɪˈvɨjɪzʲdʲɪ]): An official written recognizance of non-departure issued by an investigator under the Soviet Code of Criminal Procedure. Serving as a preventive restriction, it legally bars a suspect or key person of interest from leaving the municipal district boundaries during an active investigation. 

Nadya’s face relaxed from hearing the word ‘no’ alone.

“Now, since we’ve already wrapped up our investigation here, please join us back to the station,” he gestured Luka to a police patrol car.

Luka had no choice but to comply. Other officers followed him beside and behind him.

The scene caused many citizens to point and gossip. It sure did look like an arrest.

Noticing the band of police and the gossiping crowd, Lin noticed Luka being escorted. Concerned about his situation, she approached the band.

“Little Huo, what happened!? Why are you being—” she spoke to him in Dongbei, hoping to sneak past the officers’ ears.

But Tarasov interrupted her, “Do not obstruct the officer on duty, please, Correspondent!” 

Luka looked at her with a weary eyes but gave her an affirming nod. Lin stopped following them and look at Luka entering the patrol car and it drove away…










